c Community, and CoreCivic Properties. The company provides a range of solutions to government partners that serve the public good through corrections and detention management, a network of residential reentry centers to help address recidivism crisis, and government real estate solutions. Its correctional, detention, and residential reentry facilities offer rehabilitation and educational programs, including basic education, faith-based services, life skills and employment training, and substance abuse treatment; food services; and work and recreational programs as well as health care services, including medical, dental, and mental health services. The company was founded in 1983 and is based in Brentwood, Tennessee.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Lucibeth N. Mayberry J.D. | President, CEO & Director | Age 53 | Compensation: $1.8M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 13K people. Revenue per employee of $162K ranks in the bottom quartile among Industrials peers. Each employee generates $4K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $2K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 11\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["CoreCivic, Inc. is a mid-cap industrials business with a $2.7B market cap on $2.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +6% over the past three years and +2% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 5% against a 8% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $53M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 2 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.67\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 3.2\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 3.5\u00d7.","Management returns a meaningful 8.5% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 2.4% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Very elevated short interest (18.5% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $11.0M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Industrials sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 6% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cyclicality and capital intensity \u2014 order books turn on a dime, and the heavy fixed cost base that creates the moat also magnifies the downturn.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Supply chain reshoring costs and labour shortages \u2014 onshoring mandates raise capex requirements while a shrinking skilled-labour pool inflates operating costs.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Tariff and trade-policy exposure \u2014 industrial exporters sit squarely in the crosshairs of protectionist policy, and retaliatory tariffs can close markets without warning.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Valued at a 46% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","Piotroski score 7/9 \u2014 financials improving across profitability, leverage and efficiency","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"CoreCivic's (NYSE:CXW) Performance Raises Some Questions","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/commercial-services/nyse-cxw/corecivic/news/corecivics-nysecxw-performance-raises-some-questions"